---
import InsightArticle from '../../layouts/InsightArticle.astro';
import WorkedTable from '../../components/insights/WorkedTable.astro';
import PractitionerNote from '../../components/insights/PractitionerNote.astro';
import { practitionerB2 } from '../../data/practitioner-b2';
import examples from '../../data/insights/2026-11-14-operations.json';
const e = practitionerB2[1];
---
<InsightArticle title={e.title} description={e.description} date="2026-11-14" type="Analysis" readTime="5 min" standfirst="In a synthetic twenty-turn conversation, a 20,000-token starting prompt grows into 590,000 billed input tokens across the run. The window size and the cumulative bill answer different questions.">
  <p>A document fits inside a model's context window. That establishes capacity for one request, subject to the other material you send. It does not tell you how much a conversation about that document will cost. For that you need the sequence of requests the application actually makes.</p>
  <p>Anthropic's <a href={e.sources[0].url}>context-window guide</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes previous messages as part of later input and notes that cached prefixes still occupy the window. We use an explicit replay example here. It is an assumed application design, not a description of every chat interface.</p>
  <h2>Follow the requests, not the file</h2>
  <p>Our fictional application starts with 20,000 input tokens. It retains another 1,000 tokens between each request, representing the previous exchange. It sends the full retained history again. There is no caching, trimming or compaction. We price input at an assumed $1 per million tokens and leave output charges out.</p>
  <p>The first request sends 20,000 tokens, the second 21,000 and the third 22,000. By the twentieth request, the individual input is 39,000. Add the inputs across the whole run and you reach 590,000. That total is spend accumulated across requests, not the size of a single window.</p>
  <WorkedTable headers={['Turns', 'Cumulative replay input', 'Input bill', 'Fixed-prefix baseline tokens', 'Baseline bill']} rows={examples.context} caption="Illustrative full-history replay, input only, at an assumed $1 per million tokens. Baseline resends 20,000 tokens each turn without retaining additional history; it is not a tested substitute." />
  <p>The fixed-prefix baseline makes the source of the growth visible. At twenty turns it sends 400,000 tokens and costs $0.40. The replay run sends 190,000 more and costs $0.59. Removing that history would change what the model can see, so the difference is not an automatic saving you can take without checking the result.</p>
  <h2>A growing sum needs a different budget</h2>
  <p>For N turns, the initial prefix contributes 20,000 times N. The retained additions contribute 1,000 times the sum from zero to N minus one. That second term is 1,000 × N × (N − 1) ÷ 2. It explains why multiplying the first request by the turn count understates this particular run.</p>
  <p>Real conversations will not add exactly the same number of tokens on every turn. Some applications summarise history; others remove tool results or keep different reasoning blocks. Count actual request usage for your application rather than fitting it to this example. Keep a per-request series so that a sudden jump can be traced to a file or tool response.</p>
  <h2>Separate space, cost and usefulness</h2>
  <p>A capacity check asks whether the next request and its allowed response fit. A cost check adds billable usage across requests. A quality check asks whether the answer is supported by the material. These deserve separate columns in a tracker. Passing one does not establish the others.</p>
  <p>If you introduce caching, keep the total context count while splitting the cost into ordinary input, writes and reads. If you introduce a summary, record which material it replaces and test questions whose answers depend on that material. A smaller request is observable. Preserving the information you need requires another check.</p>
  <p>For document work, keep a few questions that depend on small details and a few whose answers are absent. Ask for the supporting passage and review it. This is a proposed test procedure, not evidence that a particular long-context model succeeds or fails.</p>
  <h2>Give the workflow a stopping rule</h2>
  <p>Set a budget for the complete job and inspect cumulative usage as it runs. Decide when to start a fresh conversation, narrow the documents or ask the user to choose a scope. Keep the point of intervention in the record so later tests compare the same workflow.</p>
  <p>A million-token headline is useful when checking capacity. Your bill comes from the requests you actually send. Track both.</p>
  <PractitionerNote evidence={e} />
</InsightArticle>
